{
  "teamId": 143,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Philadelphia Phillies were a team that spent all season auditioning for a playoff spot they never quite earned. Opening Day brought immediate optimism — Jean Segura's walk-off single in the first game of the year set an electric tone — but the bullpen, and Héctor Neris in particular, would become a recurring nightmare. April finished at a frustrating .500, and May's 12-16 skid buried them early, with Sam Coonrod and a shaky relief corps surrendering leads in heartbreaking fashion. The offense had moments of brilliance, but the pitching staff's 4.39 ERA meant no lead ever felt safe.\n\nJune arrived with a jolt. Luke Williams, a virtual unknown, crushed a walk-off two-run homer on June 9th in one of the season's most electrifying moments, and the Phillies finished the month at 12-12. But the bullpen kept bleeding — Neftalí Feliz surrendered a Nick Castellanos grand slam on June 28th that encapsulated everything frustrating about this roster. The team was good enough to tread water, not good enough to pull away.\n\nJuly and August were the Phillies at their best. A 15-12 July featured Andrew McCutchen's clutch three-run blast against Colorado and Brad Miller's pinch-hit grand slam in late July. August was even better — a 17-11 month, the team's finest stretch — powered by timely hitting from Rhys Hoskins, Brad Miller, and Jean Segura. For a brief, giddy moment, a Wild Card berth felt inevitable. The bullpen still blew games, but the offense was finally outrunning the damage.\n\nSeptember crushed those dreams. A 13-14 collapse, dotted with gut-punch bullpen meltdowns from Ian Kennedy and Archie Bradley, slowly squeezed the life out of the playoff push. The offense responded with late heroics — J.T. Realmuto's go-ahead triple, Ronald Torreyes' three-run homer, Didi Gregorius delivering in the final days — but it was all too little, too late. An 82-80 record left the Phillies one game under a winning tally with nothing to show for it, a maddening testament to what might have been had the bullpen simply held a few more leads.",
